 the minimum total number of handshakes.\n\n2. **Graph Theory Interpretation:**\n   - We can represent the party as a graph where each person is a vertex and each handshake is an edge.\n   - Each vertex must have a degree of at least 1 (each person shakes hands with at least one other person).\n\n3. **Minimizing the Number of Handshakes:**\n   - To minimize the number of handshakes, we should try to minimize the number of edges in the graph while ensuring each vertex has a degree of at least 1.\n   - In graph theory, a tree is a connected graph with no cycles and the minimum number of edges. A tree with \\( n \\) vertices has \\( n-1 \\) edges.\n\n4. **Applying the Tree Concept:**\n   - For 23 people (vertices), the minimum number of edges (handshakes) in a connected graph (tree) is \\( 23 - 1 = 22 \\).\n\n5. **Verification:**\n   - In a tree with 23 vertices, each vertex is connected to at least one other vertex, and there are exactly 22 edges.\n   - This satisfies the condition that each person shakes hands with at least one other person.\n\nThus, the minimum possible number of handshakes is \\(\\boxed{22}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{22}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
